' @fileoverview Layout constants for the subtitle management panel (#750).
'
' Mirrors extrasLayout.bs: the panel's geometry lives in one place so the
' component that RENDERS a row and the component that SIZES the list slot can
' never disagree about how tall a row is. The first draft hardcoded the slot
' height in SubtitlePanel while SubtitleResultRow derived its own from live font
' metrics; the two differed, and rows visibly drifted out of their slots while
' the search and "current subtitles" columns failed to line up.
'
' The panel's vertical extent is inherited from extrasLayout — it uses the same
' open/closed positions as the extras pane on purpose, so the two panels read as
' the same gesture.

namespace subtitleLayout

  ' Horizontal inset inside a row. Matched to TrackDropdownRow's ROW_H_PADDING so
  ' a focused subtitle row and a focused track-dropdown row read as one control
  ' family.
  const ROW_H_PADDING = 12

  ' Vertical breathing room above the first line and below the second.
  const ROW_V_PADDING = 10

  ' Gap between the row's two lines.
  const ROW_LINE_GAP = 6

  ' Gap between the detail text and the badge zone on the second line. Wide
  ' enough that an ellipsized detail line never looks joined to the first badge.
  const ROW_BADGE_GUTTER = 24

  ' Width reserved for badges at the right of a row's second line.
  '
  ' Sized by the BADGE STRINGS, not by the column: the same words are drawn
  ' whatever the column's width, and the two row types carry different sets.
  ' Measured off a device capture at fontSizeSmallest — ~10.3px per character
  ' plus 28px for an icon and its gap, 16px between badges:
  '
  '   results   "Perfect match" 162 + "Hearing impaired" 193 + "Forced" 62,
  '             with two 16px gaps = 449
  '   existing  "Hearing impaired" 193 + "Forced" 62 + one gap = 271
  '
  ' So each holds every badge its row type can carry IN ENGLISH, with a little
  ' slack. A longer translation, or a results row that also carries
  ' "Machine translated", will exceed it — and that is handled rather than
  ' prevented: badges are appended most-significant first and the zone is
  ' clipped to the row's right padding edge, so the overflow is cut instead of
  ' overrunning the neighbouring column, and what is lost is always the least
  ' significant badge. Widening these to fit every language would cost the
  ' common two-badge row a large dead gap, which is the worse trade.
  const RESULTS_BADGE_WIDTH = 460
  const EXISTING_BADGE_WIDTH = 300

  ' Floor for the detail text so a hypothetical narrow row can never be handed a
  ' negative width (a Label with width <= 0 renders its text unclipped).
  const ROW_DETAIL_MIN_WIDTH = 120

  ' Column widths. The results column is wide because release names run long —
  ' 85 characters was the longest observed in a real result set — and width here
  ' directly buys fewer marquees.
  const RESULTS_WIDTH = 1120
  const EXISTING_WIDTH = 570

  ' Rows visible in either column without scrolling. Drives the MarkupList's
  ' numRows AND the perfect-match filter's visibility threshold — the filter is
  ' pointless when the whole result set already fits on screen, so the two must
  ' be the same number by construction.
  const VISIBLE_ROWS = 6

  ' rowHeight: Total height of one subtitle row.
  '
  ' Exactly two lines, and that is a shape decision rather than an arithmetic
  ' one: the first draft reserved a third line for a badge strip on EVERY row so
  ' both columns would stay a uniform height, which left rows without badges
  ' looking broken next to rows with them. Badges now sit on the second line
  ' beside the detail text, so a badge-free row is correctly spaced by
  ' construction and the reserved strip is gone.
  '
  ' Pure by construction — the font sizes are passed in rather than read off
  ' m.global — so it is unit-testable and callable from either component's
  ' scope.
  '
  ' @param {integer} fontSmaller - constants.fontSizeSmaller (first line)
  ' @param {integer} fontSmallest - constants.fontSizeSmallest (second line)
  ' @return {integer} row height in pixels
  function rowHeight(fontSmaller as integer, fontSmallest as integer) as integer
    return (subtitleLayout.ROW_V_PADDING * 2) + fontSmaller + subtitleLayout.ROW_LINE_GAP + fontSmallest
  end function

  ' secondLineZones: Where the detail text and the badges go on a row's second line.
  '
  ' Returned together, from one function, because the two are complementary
  ' halves of the same split — computing them separately is how the panel's
  ' first draft ended up with a row component and a list slot that disagreed.
  '
  ' The badge zone is anchored to the row's right padding edge rather than
  ' flowing after the detail text. Flowing would waste nothing, but the detail
  ' line's width swings widely (uploader names in the sampled results ran from 4
  ' to 20 characters), so a flowed badge would land at a different x on every
  ' row and read as jitter while scrolling. A fixed anchor also means the row
  ' needs no text measurement, which a MarkupList row cannot do cheaply — it is
  ' recycled on every scroll step and localBoundingRect() is only valid after a
  ' render pass.
  '
  ' @param {integer} rowWidth - full width of the row
  ' @param {integer} badgeZoneWidth - width to reserve for badges (RESULTS_/EXISTING_BADGE_WIDTH)
  ' @return {object} { detailWidth, badgeX, badgeWidth }
  function secondLineZones(rowWidth as integer, badgeZoneWidth as integer) as object
    usable = rowWidth - (subtitleLayout.ROW_H_PADDING * 2)

    ' Badges give way to the detail text, not the other way round: the text is
    ' the row's content and the badges only qualify it.
    badgeWidth = badgeZoneWidth
    maxBadgeWidth = usable - subtitleLayout.ROW_BADGE_GUTTER - subtitleLayout.ROW_DETAIL_MIN_WIDTH
    if badgeWidth > maxBadgeWidth then badgeWidth = maxBadgeWidth
    if badgeWidth < 0 then badgeWidth = 0

    ' No badge zone means no gap to leave before it — the detail line takes the
    ' whole row rather than ending short of an edge that isn't there.
    gutter = subtitleLayout.ROW_BADGE_GUTTER
    if badgeWidth = 0 then gutter = 0

    detailWidth = usable - badgeWidth - gutter
    ' A Label with width <= 0 renders UNCLIPPED, so the degenerate row has to end
    ' up with a positive width even when it is too narrow to honour the floor.
    if detailWidth < 1 then detailWidth = 1

    return {
      detailWidth: detailWidth,
      badgeX: rowWidth - subtitleLayout.ROW_H_PADDING - badgeWidth,
      badgeWidth: badgeWidth
    }
  end function

end namespace
